1. Advantages of using Mongoose over Native MongoDB Driver

Mongoose is an ODM (Object Data Modeling) library for Node.js that adds structure and features on top of MongoDB.

Key Advantages:
Schema-based structure → Define clear models for data
Validation → Built-in validation rules (required, min, max, etc.)
Middleware (hooks) → Run logic before/after operations
Easier queries → Cleaner and more readable syntax
Population → Easy handling of relationships between collections
Default values & setters/getters → More control over data

👉 Native driver is more flexible but requires more manual handling.

2. Difference between findOneAndUpdate() and updateOne()
Feature	findOneAndUpdate()	updateOne()
Returns	Updated document	Update result (acknowledgment)
Default behavior	Returns old doc (can return new with option)	Does NOT return document
Use case	When you need updated data immediately	When you just want to update
Middleware	Triggers certain hooks	Limited middleware
Example:
// findOneAndUpdate
await User.findOneAndUpdate(
  { name: "Akhil" },
  { age: 22 },
  { new: true } // returns updated doc
);

// updateOne
await User.updateOne(
  { name: "Akhil" },
  { age: 22 }
);
3. Purpose of Middleware in Mongoose

Middleware (also called hooks) are functions that run before or after certain operations.

Types:
Pre middleware → runs before action
Post middleware → runs after action
Example:
userSchema.pre("save", function(next) {
  console.log("Before saving user");
  next();
});
Purpose:
Data validation
Logging
Encryption (e.g., hashing passwords)
Business logic execution
4. Pagination in Mongoose

Pagination is used to fetch data in chunks/pages instead of all at once.

Basic Implementation:
const page = 2;
const limit = 5;

const users = await User.find()
  .skip((page - 1) * limit)
  .limit(limit);
Explanation:
skip() → skips previous records
limit() → number of records per page

👉 Example: Page 2 with 5 records → skips first 5, returns next 5

5. Embedding vs Referencing in MongoDB
Embedding (Nested Documents)

Store related data inside the same document.

Use when:

Data is closely related
Read operations are frequent
Data size is small

Example:

{
  name: "Akhil",
  address: {
    city: "Delhi",
    pincode: 110001
  }
}
Referencing (Using ObjectId)

Store reference (ID) of another document.

Use when:

Data is large
Data is reused across multiple documents
Frequent updates needed

Example:

{
  name: "Akhil",
  courseId: ObjectId("123abc")
}
Quick Difference:
Embedding	Referencing
Faster reads	Better for large/complex data
No joins needed	Requires population
Limited size	Scalable
